// GetLoadStats returns data-loading statistics accumulated since process start.
//
// Unlike the other RPCs here, the payload is JSON rather than typed fields.
// This is a human-readable diagnostic report whose field set is expected to grow,
// and JSON keeps that growth free for the Rust/Python SDKs (which pass the bytes
// through untouched) instead of costing a hand-written mapping in each of them.
//
// payload is UTF-8 JSON with the schema below. Fields may be added, but are
// never removed or renamed:
//
//	{
//	  "uptime_seconds": 3600,           // process uptime, the denominator for every counter below
//	  "source": {
//	    "reads": 12345,                 // source reads (blocks fetched from the source filesystem)
//	    "bytes": 51539607552,           // bytes read from source
//	    "errors": 2,                    // failed source reads
//	    "in_flight": 3,                 // source reads in progress right now
//	    "sourceless_retry": 0,          // peer-load retries in sourceless meta-consumer mode
//	    "sourceless_retry_bytes": 0,
//	    "latency": {                    // successful source reads only, see note below
//	      "count": 12343,
//	      "sum": 185.1,                 // seconds
//	      "buckets": [ {"le": 0.01, "n": 4000} ]  // n is cumulative: observations <= le
//	    }
//	  },
//	  "peer": {
//	    "hits": 8123,                   // peer loads that returned data
//	    "miss": 412,                    // peer loads where every candidate failed; the read fell through to source
//	    "hit_bytes": 33285996544,
//	    "miss_bytes": 1717986918,
//	    "timeouts": 1180,               // per-peer read timeouts; can far exceed miss, since one
//	                                    // miss may have timed out against many peers
//	    "blacklisted": 7,               // peers skipped because they were blacklisted
//	    "checksum_errors": 0,
//	    "in_flight": 1,
//	    "latency": { }                  // successful peer loads only, same shape as source.latency
//	  }
//	}
//
// Things to know before drawing conclusions from this:
//
//  1. reads and hits count successes only. Failures go to errors and miss
//     respectively and are never added to them, and the latency histograms are
//     observed on the same success paths, so latency.count tracks reads / hits
//     rather than falling short of them. Everything except errors/miss
//     describes the successful subset.
//  2. Time burned by a load that ultimately failed is recorded nowhere.
//     timeouts and blacklisted indicate that waste exists, but it cannot be
//     converted into seconds.
